{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["Scripture says here <b>\"And you shall command\"</b> because in the matter of the Tabernacle it always says \"ועשית\" (and you shall make), which is also a commandment. Here it says ואתה — you yourself shall command them that they bring to you the oil for the luminary, for they have no way to make it in the wilderness unless they had it in reserve from before. And the princes brought it (below, Exodus 35:27–28)." "The sense of <b>\"to you\"</b> is that they shall bring it before him and he shall inspect it to see whether it is pure and beaten as required. So too, \"Command the children of Israel that they bring to you\" (Leviticus 24:2)." "The sense of <b>\"from the children of Israel\"</b> is that they shall bring you the oil from the children of Israel — from whatever is found in their hands, as He said, \"from every man whose heart moves him\" (Exodus 25:2). So too He said, \"And you, bring near to you Aaron your brother\" (Exodus 28:1) — for you yourself shall call them and inform them of this elevation. And the continuation, \"and you shall make for them holy garments\" (Exodus 28:2), is a commandment to the wise men who do the work, in the manner of all the labor of the pattern. And so too, \"And you shall speak to all who are wise of heart\" (Exodus 28:3) — that he himself shall speak with them, for he will recognize their wisdom and know the craft suited to be entrusted to each one of them. And because it says \"And you, bring near\" and \"and you shall make,\" it was necessary to explain (Exodus 28:3) \"And you shall speak to all who are wise of heart,\" for by commandment they shall make the garments like the rest of the work concerning which it says, \"See, I have called by name Betzalel,\" etc. (Exodus 31:2)." "<b>\"To cause a lamp to burn continually\"</b> — every night and night is called \"continually\" (תמיד), as you say, \"a continual burnt offering\" (Numbers 28:6), and it is nothing but from day to day. So too with the meal offering of flatbreads it says \"continually\" (Leviticus 6:13), and it is nothing but half of it in the morning and half of it in the evening. But the \"continually\" stated regarding the bread of the Presence (Exodus 25:30) is from Sabbath to Sabbath. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 27:20). But the midrash of our Sages is not so; rather, thus we learned in Sifrei (beginning of Beha'alotekha): \"\"The seven lamps shall give light\" (Numbers 8:2) — I might think they burn forever; Scripture says, \"from evening to morning\" (Leviticus 24:3). If from evening to morning, one might think they burn from evening to morning and then he extinguishes them; Scripture says, \"the seven lamps shall give light.\" How so? The seven lamps shall give light from evening to morning. \"Before HaShem continually\" (Leviticus 24:3) — that the western lamp burn constantly, for from it they light the menorah at dusk. And in Torat Kohanim (Leviticus 24:2) as well they said thus: \"To cause a lamp to burn continually\" — that the western lamp be constant. And they taught there: if one found it extinguished, he clears it and lights it from the altar of burnt offering — for even during the day one lights the western lamp, because it is continual forever.\nAnd in tractate Tamid (3:9) we learned thus: he who won the lot for clearing the menorah enters and finds the two eastern lamps burning; he clears the rest and leaves those burning in their place. If he found them extinguished, he clears them and lights them from the burning ones, and afterward clears the rest. And this anonymous Mishnah is taught according to the words of Rabbi (Menahot 98b), who said that the lamps were arranged east and west. According to his view, the western lamp is the second one, and it is called western because it is west of the first; and since he needs to light the western lamp to fulfill in it \"before HaShem continually,\" he must light the eastern one as well — for the second is not called western unless that one is eastern relative to it. But according to the one who says (there) that they were arranged north and south, the western lamp is the middle one in the body of the menorah, and it alone is lit in the morning. And in general, \"before HaShem continually\" refers to the western lamp, which burns continually by day and by night."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:19:03.912216801Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}